## Project Overview

**@hiprax/crypto** is a high-security encryption/decryption library for Node.js, published on NPM under the `@hiprax/crypto` scope. It provides AES-256-GCM authenticated encryption with Argon2id (async) or PBKDF2 (sync) key derivation, full TypeScript support, and both text and file encryption capabilities.

- **Package**: `@hiprax/crypto` (v1.4.1)
- **Author**: Hiprax
- **License**: MIT
- **Repository**: [GitHub](https://github.com/Hiprax/crypto)
- **NPM**: [NPM](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@hiprax/crypto)
- **Node.js**: >=22.0.0 (Node 18 EOL'd 2025-04-30, Node 20 EOL'd 2026-04-30; we target maintenance + active LTS only as of v1.3.0)
- **Optional runtime dependencies**: `argon2` (native addon via node-gyp) and `hash-wasm` (pure-WASM fallback); both listed in `optionalDependencies`. Async paths try native `argon2` first and fall back transparently to `hash-wasm` if native is unavailable. Sync paths use PBKDF2 and need neither.
- **Module system**: ESM-only (`"type": "module"` in package.json; `exports` map has no `require` key, so CJS consumers must use dynamic `import()`)

---

## Architecture

### Source Files

```text
src/
├── index.ts              # Entry point — re-exports everything
├── crypto-manager.ts     # Main CryptoManager class (all encryption logic)
├── format.ts             # Versioned "HPCR" wire-format: buildHeader/parseHeader/inspectHeader, DoS caps
├── types.ts              # All interfaces, enums, and CryptoError class
├── utils.ts              # Standalone utility functions
└── __tests__/
    ├── crypto-manager.test.ts    # CryptoManager test suite
    ├── utils.test.ts             # Utility functions test suite
    ├── argon2-lazy-load.test.ts  # Lazy-load fallback for missing argon2
    ├── esm-smoke.test.ts         # ESM-only export-shape smoke test
    ├── property.test.ts          # fast-check property-based tests (Task 19 + Task 7 tampering)
    ├── format-fuzz.test.ts       # fast-check fuzzing harness for parseHeader / inspectHeader (Task 12)
    ├── format-snapshot.test.ts   # v1 wire-format snapshot tests (Task 13)
    ├── streaming-cleanup.test.ts # streaming failure-path coverage (Task 18)
    └── __snapshots__/
        └── format-snapshot.test.ts.snap  # checked-in byte-layout snapshots
```

### Benchmarks (`bench/`)

```text
bench/
├── README.md            # Per-case methodology, expected runtime breakdown
├── index.mjs            # Top-level runner — sequentially imports the three child files
├── kdf.mjs              # Argon2id (default 128 MiB / t=3 / p=1) + PBKDF2-SHA256 (600k iter)
├── encrypt-text.mjs     # encryptText 1 KiB + encryptText 1 MiB
└── encrypt-file.mjs     # encryptFile 10 MiB + decryptFile 10 MiB (streaming round-trip)
```

ESM-only (`.mjs`), driven by `tinybench@^6.0.1` (devDependency). Runs against the compiled `dist/` output, not the TypeScript source — `npm run build` is a prerequisite. Wired into `package.json` as `npm run bench`. Excluded from the npm tarball via `.npmignore`. Not in CI — Argon2id at 128 MiB is too slow and the absolute numbers vary too much between runner generations to be useful as a regression check. Total runtime ~2-5 minutes on a modern laptop at the production 128 MiB profile.

### Entry Point (`src/index.ts`)

- Named exports: `CryptoManager`, `SECURITY_THRESHOLDS`, `isValidPassword` from `./crypto-manager.js`
- Default export: `CryptoManager`
- Wildcard re-exports from `./utils.js`, `./types.js`, and `./format.js`
- Import paths use `.js` extensions (required for ESM with TypeScript)

### Package Exports (package.json)

Four export paths are defined; each declares only `types` + `import` (no `require` key — the package is ESM-only):

- `"."` → `dist/index.js` (main entry)
- `"./crypto-manager"` → `dist/crypto-manager.js`
- `"./utils"` → `dist/utils.js`
- `"./package.json"` → `./package.json` (for tooling that resolves the manifest under the new exports map)

CJS consumers cannot rely on `require('@hiprax/crypto')` — the missing `"require"` key in the exports map means Node has no synchronous CJS resolution path. On Node 22+ (the current minimum) `require(esm)` is permitted by default for ESM packages with no top-level await, so a synchronous-looking `require` call may actually succeed via Node's synthetic-namespace path; the supported and portable interop is still `await import('@hiprax/crypto')` from inside an `async` function in a CJS file (the only pattern the test suite verifies). See README's "CommonJS interop" section.

### Argon2 lazy-load (`src/crypto-manager.ts`)

Both `argon2` (native) and `hash-wasm` (pure-WASM) live in `optionalDependencies`, NOT `dependencies`. Async key derivation uses a unified `Argon2Hasher` interface so that the rest of the code is provider-agnostic. The provider is selected by the private `importArgon2Hasher()` function, which tries native `argon2` first, then falls back to `hash-wasm`; both implement the RFC 9106 Argon2id reference and produce bit-identical raw output for the same parameters, so v1 ciphertexts round-trip across both providers without any migration step.

The resolved provider is cached at module scope using an in-flight-promise pattern (since v1.1.1):

- Initial: `argon2ModuleCache = null` — load not yet attempted, OR previous attempt rejected.
- During load: cached as the in-flight `Promise<Argon2Hasher>` so concurrent first-callers share one `importArgon2Hasher()` call (no duplicate dynamic imports under load).
- On success: the resolved promise stays cached forever; subsequent callers `await` an already-settled promise (no re-import).
- On rejection: the cache slot is cleared (via a compare-and-swap check that only nulls the slot if it still holds *our* failing promise); the NEXT caller starts a fresh load. Transient failures (e.g. ephemeral FS permission errors during a parallel build-tool install) recover within the same process.

The Argon2id type identifier (numeric `2`) is hardcoded in a module-level constant `ARGON2_ID` so the constructor never needs to touch any Argon2 module — `new CryptoManager()` works regardless of whether either optional package is installed.

When BOTH providers are unavailable, `loadArgon2()` throws `CryptoError(MEMORY_ERROR, 'ARGON2_NOT_AVAILABLE')` with the actionable message:

> `argon2 native module unavailable. Install build tools (Python + node-gyp) or install the optional \`hash-wasm\` package for a pure-WASM Argon2id fallback (slower than native but works everywhere). Alternatively, use *Sync methods (PBKDF2). Native error: <msg>. WASM error: <msg>.`

For test purposes only, `crypto-manager.ts` exports `__resetArgon2ModuleCacheForTesting()` which clears the module-level cache. Tests use `jest.unstable_mockModule('argon2', factory)` and `jest.unstable_mockModule('hash-wasm', factory)` BEFORE dynamically `import()`-ing CryptoManager so the lazy imports resolve to mocked modules.

